1
00:00:00,010 --> 00:00:01,740
You can't see through sand.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,730
So how are you looking
through it right now?

3
00:00:05,230 --> 00:00:07,920
Your phone screen, your
window, that glass of water.

4
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:09,570
It's mostly melted sand.

5
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:11,700
And the first glass people ever held?

6
00:00:12,210 --> 00:00:12,890
Nobody made it.

7
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:16,130
Take that yellow beetle in
a young king's jewelry.

8
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:18,960
It's glass, about 29 million years old.

9
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:22,260
So how did humans turn
sand into clear glass?

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,320
That beetle's glass most likely formed
when a space rock hit the desert.

11
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:29,310
People only had fire.

12
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:33,580
Along the way, there's
a how-to guide in clay.

13
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:36,640
Step one: pick a lucky month.

14
00:00:37,290 --> 00:00:41,480
There's the world's favorite glass story,
which its own writer passed on as a rumor.

15
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:45,360
And there's one breath that
made everyday glass cheap.

16
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:48,340
Each step adds a piece to this puzzle.

17
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:50,720
Fill it in, and you'll see the answer.

18
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:53,380
So where did that beetle come from?

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,880
In 1922, Howard Carter found
Tutankhamun's treasures.

20
00:00:58,020 --> 00:01:01,490
He listed the beetle as
chalcedony, a kind of stone.

21
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:07,790
In 1998, an Italian mineral expert,
Vincenzo de Michele, took a closer look.

22
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:09,490
It wasn't stone at all.

23
00:01:09,890 --> 00:01:12,600
This was desert glass, from western Egypt.

24
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:16,160
Chunks of it lie across
thousands of square kilometers.

25
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:21,060
It's canary yellow and nearly pure
silica, the main stuff in sand.

26
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:24,170
And it formed about 29 million years ago.

27
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,330
In 2019, scientists found tiny minerals
that only form in a huge impact.

28
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:34,030
The best bet: a space rock hit the ground.

29
00:01:35,030 --> 00:01:38,220
Nature made glass another
way too: volcanoes.

30
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,190
Volcanic glass is called obsidian,
and it's black and shiny.

31
00:01:42,710 --> 00:01:47,328
More than 8,000 years ago, people at
Çatalhöyük, in today's Turkey, polished it into

32
00:01:47,328 --> 00:01:47,770
mirrors.

33
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:49,590
Some still give a sharp reflection.

34
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:54,770
Your bathroom mirror has a very
old, very volcanic ancestor.

35
00:01:55,390 --> 00:01:56,400
But here's the catch.

36
00:01:56,670 --> 00:01:58,950
People could shape natural
glass and polish it.

37
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:01,430
They couldn't melt it, or make any more.

38
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:03,680
First piece: NATURE.

39
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:06,920
People knew glass long
before they could make it.

40
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:09,620
So who made the first glass by hand?

41
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:12,730
Probably someone who was
trying to make something else.

42
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:15,600
Nobody set out to invent glass.

43
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:17,410
It probably started as a coating.

44
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:22,220
About 6,000 years ago, craftspeople
made something called faience.

45
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:26,310
It's crushed quartz with a
glassy skin, often bright blue.

46
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:30,590
That skin was a glaze, a bit like
the shine on your coffee mug.

47
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:34,730
The Met museum calls it an
early forerunner of glass.

48
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:40,990
Then, about 4,500 years ago, the
oldest glass made by people shows up.

49
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:45,800
Small beads, most likely from
Mesopotamia, around today's Iraq.

50
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:47,240
How did people get there?

51
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:51,020
Nobody knows for sure, but
there are good guesses.

52
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:54,520
Maybe a glaze got so thick that
it could stand on its own.

53
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:58,130
Or maybe it was a leftover
from metalworking fires.

54
00:02:58,810 --> 00:03:02,130
The Getty Museum says it was
possibly a very fortunate accident.

55
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:04,790
Piece two: ACCIDENT.

56
00:03:05,630 --> 00:03:09,200
Glass most likely began as
a side effect, not a plan.

57
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:12,670
For about a thousand years,
glass beads stayed rare.

58
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:14,380
Then kings got involved.

59
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:17,010
And that's where the
paperwork gets strange.

60
00:03:17,941 --> 00:03:20,701
Egypt, about 3,350 years ago.

61
00:03:21,141 --> 00:03:23,471
A pharaoh sends word that he wants glass.

62
00:03:24,111 --> 00:03:28,391
The reply comes on a clay tablet, from a
ruler on the eastern Mediterranean coast.

63
00:03:28,791 --> 00:03:32,481
His name is Yidya, and he's
sending 30 pieces of glass.

64
00:03:32,951 --> 00:03:34,731
Thirty pieces, for a king.

65
00:03:35,311 --> 00:03:37,071
That's how precious glass still was.

66
00:03:37,921 --> 00:03:39,261
Egypt also made its own.

67
00:03:40,031 --> 00:03:43,171
One glass factory stood at
Qantir, in the Nile Delta.

68
00:03:43,871 --> 00:03:48,311
In 2005, researchers Thilo Rehren and
Edgar Pusch described how it worked.

69
00:03:48,741 --> 00:03:50,861
And one surprise: no sand.

70
00:03:51,441 --> 00:03:55,971
Workers crushed quartz pebbles
and heated them in old clay jars.

71
00:03:56,511 --> 00:03:59,941
Then they colored the glass
and cast it into round blocks.

72
00:04:00,531 --> 00:04:03,251
Those blocks are called
ingots, and they traveled far.

73
00:04:04,221 --> 00:04:10,252
About 3,300 years ago, a trading ship sank
off today's Turkey, carrying blue glass

74
00:04:10,252 --> 00:04:10,771
blocks.

75
00:04:11,231 --> 00:04:13,931
Tests show some of that
glass was made in Egypt.

76
00:04:14,391 --> 00:04:16,591
It was probably on its
way to ancient Greece.

77
00:04:17,621 --> 00:04:19,481
But how did people pass on the recipe?

78
00:04:20,261 --> 00:04:21,641
They wrote it down, in clay.

79
00:04:22,151 --> 00:04:25,381
The best-known copies come from
a royal library at Nineveh.

80
00:04:25,821 --> 00:04:30,601
They're about 2,700 years old,
and the recipe inside is older.

81
00:04:31,411 --> 00:04:32,451
So what's step one?

82
00:04:33,031 --> 00:04:36,481
When you build the glass furnace,
pick a good day in a favorable month.

83
00:04:37,181 --> 00:04:38,991
Step two: keep strangers out.

84
00:04:39,581 --> 00:04:42,931
My flat-pack shelf starts
with: check all the screws.

85
00:04:43,591 --> 00:04:44,991
This one starts with a calendar.

86
00:04:45,501 --> 00:04:47,151
Piece three: RECIPE.

87
00:04:47,861 --> 00:04:51,701
Glass now had a written recipe,
and kings asked for it by letter.

88
00:04:52,131 --> 00:04:53,791
But where did glass first come from?

89
00:04:54,121 --> 00:04:55,301
Rome had a story for that.

90
00:04:56,331 --> 00:04:58,491
Here's the most famous glass story of all.

91
00:04:59,491 --> 00:05:03,071
Pliny the Elder, a Roman
writer, told it around AD 77.

92
00:05:04,111 --> 00:05:07,371
Sailors carrying blocks of
soda land on a sandy beach.

93
00:05:07,751 --> 00:05:11,311
With no rocks around, they rest
their cooking pots on soda lumps.

94
00:05:11,711 --> 00:05:12,801
The fire roars.

95
00:05:12,981 --> 00:05:16,721
Soon the soda mixes with the sand,
and clear streams start to flow.

96
00:05:17,451 --> 00:05:21,001
The soda block, if it could talk:
I was only holding up a pot!

97
00:05:21,591 --> 00:05:24,161
But look at how Pliny
starts: the story goes.

98
00:05:24,471 --> 00:05:25,771
Even he wasn't sure.

99
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:28,931
Most experts doubt a beach
cooking fire could do it.

100
00:05:29,911 --> 00:05:33,281
Ancient glassmakers needed
furnaces running hot for hours.

101
00:05:33,941 --> 00:05:37,261
And glass was already more than
2,000 years old when Pliny wrote.

102
00:05:38,391 --> 00:05:43,988
Subscribe if you like myths checked like
this — next up, how prehistoric humans told

103
00:05:43,988 --> 00:05:44,331
time.

104
00:05:45,061 --> 00:05:48,001
Still, the story gets
one thing right: soda.

105
00:05:48,831 --> 00:05:52,451
Sand alone needs about 1,700
degrees Celsius to melt.

106
00:05:52,811 --> 00:05:55,701
Ancient glass furnaces
ran much cooler than that.

107
00:05:56,431 --> 00:05:59,781
Add soda, and the mix starts
to melt at around 1,000.

108
00:06:00,681 --> 00:06:05,051
Chemists call soda a flux, a helper
that makes things melt at lower heat.

109
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:07,731
It was the ancient glassmaker's shortcut.

110
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:11,231
So why is sand dull, but glass clear?

111
00:06:12,121 --> 00:06:13,951
Every grain of sand has edges.

112
00:06:14,321 --> 00:06:18,451
Billions of edges bounce light around,
so you can't see through a pile.

113
00:06:19,141 --> 00:06:22,611
Melt the grains into one smooth
piece, and the edges disappear.

114
00:06:23,071 --> 00:06:24,121
Light goes straight through.

115
00:06:24,751 --> 00:06:27,271
So that's why a window is
clear, and a beach isn't.

116
00:06:28,281 --> 00:06:28,881
Piece four: SODA.

117
00:06:29,471 --> 00:06:33,621
Before Pliny's time, glass
was rare and slow to make.

118
00:06:34,251 --> 00:06:36,011
That was about to change, with one breath.

119
00:06:37,041 --> 00:06:40,421
Until then, making a glass
bottle was slow and fiddly.

120
00:06:41,371 --> 00:06:44,011
Wind hot glass around
a clay core on a rod.

121
00:06:44,751 --> 00:06:46,991
Let it cool, then scrape the clay out.

122
00:06:47,631 --> 00:06:50,321
It worked, but only for
small bottles like this one.

123
00:06:51,811 --> 00:06:56,191
Then, a little over 2,000 years ago,
someone on the eastern Mediterranean coast

124
00:06:56,191 --> 00:06:57,311
tried something new.

125
00:06:57,981 --> 00:06:59,621
This is the biggest leap in our story.

126
00:07:00,521 --> 00:07:01,911
Imagine you're in that workshop.

127
00:07:02,681 --> 00:07:05,881
You dip a long, hollow pipe
into a blob of hot glass.

128
00:07:06,351 --> 00:07:07,091
Then you blow.

129
00:07:07,911 --> 00:07:10,621
The glass swells into a
bubble, like chewing gum.

130
00:07:11,681 --> 00:07:15,621
In Jerusalem, archaeologists found
the oldest blown glass we can date.

131
00:07:16,061 --> 00:07:19,211
It's workshop waste
from about 50 to 40 BC.

132
00:07:20,041 --> 00:07:23,941
Blowing was easier and cheaper,
and it spread west to Italy.

133
00:07:25,131 --> 00:07:29,851
In Rome, says the Greek writer Strabo,
a glass cup costs one copper coin.

134
00:07:30,471 --> 00:07:32,971
That's the Roman version
of a one-dollar glass.

135
00:07:33,561 --> 00:07:37,781
More than a thousand years earlier,
a pharaoh ordered glass by the piece.

136
00:07:38,171 --> 00:07:40,221
Fancy glass could still cost a fortune.

137
00:07:40,831 --> 00:07:43,141
Romans even put glass in some windows.

138
00:07:43,561 --> 00:07:45,361
But don't picture a modern window.

139
00:07:45,981 --> 00:07:48,001
Roman panes were thick and greenish.

140
00:07:48,251 --> 00:07:51,291
Some windows used thin slices
of a clear mineral instead.

141
00:07:52,591 --> 00:07:53,681
Piece five: BREATH.

142
00:07:54,341 --> 00:07:57,231
One breath turned glass from
a royal gift into a cheap cup.

143
00:07:57,851 --> 00:08:00,331
But clear, flat glass was still far off.

144
00:08:00,891 --> 00:08:02,111
For that, we need an island.

145
00:08:03,141 --> 00:08:04,701
Venice, 1291.

146
00:08:05,591 --> 00:08:09,171
The city's Great Council orders
its glass furnaces out of town.

147
00:08:09,761 --> 00:08:11,661
Their official reason: fire.

148
00:08:12,691 --> 00:08:15,911
So the furnaces moved to
Murano, a small island nearby.

149
00:08:16,551 --> 00:08:19,681
Secrecy came with it: glassmakers
were banned from working abroad.

150
00:08:20,351 --> 00:08:22,601
Today, a company just
makes you sign a form.

151
00:08:23,291 --> 00:08:28,151
In the 1450s, Murano glassmaker Angelo
Barovier cleaned up his recipe.

152
00:08:28,831 --> 00:08:31,831
He purified the burned plant
ash he used as his soda.

153
00:08:32,391 --> 00:08:37,821
The glass came out so clear, Venetians
named it after rock crystal: cristallo.

154
00:08:38,391 --> 00:08:40,921
But flat window glass
was still hard to make.

155
00:08:41,881 --> 00:08:44,831
Getting it truly smooth
meant grinding and polishing.

156
00:08:45,811 --> 00:08:50,591
In 1952, in Britain, Alastair Pilkington
started a strange experiment.

157
00:08:51,361 --> 00:08:54,421
He let hot liquid glass float
on a bath of melted tin.

158
00:08:54,781 --> 00:08:57,541
It came out flat and smooth on both sides.

159
00:08:57,891 --> 00:08:59,691
No grinding, no polishing.

160
00:09:00,411 --> 00:09:02,671
His company announced it in 1959.

161
00:09:03,281 --> 00:09:05,951
Today, about 500 float
lines run around the world.

162
00:09:06,901 --> 00:09:10,841
Back in 2014, how much new glass
did the world make per person?

163
00:09:11,441 --> 00:09:14,321
About 2 kilos, 21, or 200?

164
00:09:14,881 --> 00:09:15,561
Take a guess.

165
00:09:18,721 --> 00:09:20,551
About 21 kilos each.

166
00:09:21,011 --> 00:09:24,441
That's like a heavy suitcase of new
glass, for every person on Earth.

167
00:09:25,461 --> 00:09:27,111
Piece six: CLEAR.

168
00:09:28,141 --> 00:09:32,011
Venice made glass clearer, and a
tin bath made it perfectly flat.

169
00:09:32,781 --> 00:09:35,571
So, how did humans turn
sand into clear glass?

170
00:09:36,361 --> 00:09:36,891
Slowly.

171
00:09:37,471 --> 00:09:40,481
Nature made it first, and craftspeople
probably stumbled onto it.

172
00:09:41,071 --> 00:09:45,729
Then, over 4,000 years, people learned
to melt it, blow it and make it crystal

173
00:09:45,729 --> 00:09:46,091
clear.

174
00:09:46,971 --> 00:09:49,031
That yellow beetle needed a space rock.

175
00:09:49,681 --> 00:09:52,871
The glass in front of you took
thousands of years of people trying.

176
00:09:54,001 --> 00:09:55,091
One more thing from Pliny.

177
00:09:55,691 --> 00:09:57,911
He heard a rumor about
glass that could bend.

178
00:09:58,831 --> 00:10:02,111
But he called that story more
widespread than certain.

179
00:10:02,701 --> 00:10:06,691
In 2012, a glass company
showed glass you can bend.

180
00:10:07,251 --> 00:10:09,361
It's about as thin as a sheet of paper.

181
00:10:09,991 --> 00:10:10,731
Sorry, Pliny.

182
00:10:11,131 --> 00:10:14,041
Next: how prehistoric humans told time.

183
00:10:14,701 --> 00:10:16,171
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

184
00:10:16,471 --> 00:10:18,851
And what's the oldest
glass thing in your home?

185
00:10:19,491 --> 00:10:20,441
Tell me in the comments.
